the Emmy award-winning documentary  series making a murderer brought  international attention to a homicide  case in Wisconsin Steven Avery and  Brendan Dassey are serving prison  sentences for the 2005 murder of a  photographer Teresa Halbach the 10 part  series paints a picture of what some say  was severe misconduct by investigators  and it also raises questions about the  fairness of the convictions now this is  not the first controversy surrounding a  case involving Steven Avery DNA evidence  exonerated Steven Avery back in 2003 18  years after going to prison for a rape  he did not commit there isn't one iota  of physical evidence in this case the  connect Steven Avery to it but two years  into his freedom  Avery became the focus of the murder  investigation of photographer Teresa  Halbach the evidence that we collected  is leading us to that of a human person  ironically haul box last stop Monday was  it Steven Avery's home haul box car with  Avery's blood in it was discovered in  his family salvage yard her cremated  remains were found steps from his  trailer a breeze defense argued some  investigators who helped wrongfully  convicted for rape planted evidence and  forced Avery's learning-disabled nephew  Brendan Dassey into a false confession  shot her in half it's not a documentary  at all it's a advocacy piece former  District Attorney Ken Kratz told CBS  this morning the series ignores  incriminating evidence that was  presented in the trial it does very  little to help the search for the truth  that's a function that is uniquely given  to jurors and the jurors did that when  they returned their verdicts we the jury  find the defendant Steven a Avery guilty  of first-degree intentional homicide we  spoke to a Brees former defense lawyers  in 2016 there both of you convinced of  his innocence I'm not convinced of his  guilt you're saying there there is some  doubt in your mind sure apps  and if it was okay to can convict people  on maybes I wouldn't be worried about  this but it's not Forbes called the  documentary Netflix most significant  show ever part two picks up with Steven  Avery and Britain and a cease  post-conviction legal fight viewers are  also introduced to Avery's new  high-profile attorney her name is  Catherine  Kathleen rather zelner every crime makes  sense every crime can be unraveled you  can figure it out you have to understand  the victim you have to understand the  nature of the crime and you must  understand in intimate detail the crime  scene and I don't think any of that was  done in this case first on CBS this  morning filmmakers Laura Ricciardi and  Moira demos join us at the table to  discuss good to see you guys so I'm  curious about took part two because they  had both been convicted their appeals  have been overturned why would you even  think there is a part two to this case  Laura well we knew that both Stephen and  Brendan although they're serving life in  prison  Stephen without the possibility of  parole Brendan will be eligible for  parole in 2048 at the age of 59 they  continue to maintain their innocence and  they're committed to fighting to free  themselves and clear their names were  you planning to always do a part two no  it's so definitely something that  evolved how did it evolve what did you  see that you thought okay we need to  keep going Moira so you know when we  leave off at the end of part one you  know Stephen himself is saying he's  going to keep fighting  Brendan's team has actually just taken  his case into the federal court system  and then soon after the launch of Part 1  Stephen got a new advocate Kathleen  Zellner who has an incredible record he  does she's the winningest private  post-conviction attorney in the United  States and so right there it was clear  you know the story really is not over  mm-hmm so as soon as we learned that and  thought about this post conviction phase  of the process which is really kind of a  black hole to most people like what  exactly does that mean  to appeal what are the obstacles what  are the stakes what are your chances so  we saw it as an opportunity  to you know offer viewers something new  in part one as popular as it was didn't  go without criticism in controversy  there had been accusations of it being  one sided right that you didn't get  enough information in time with the  prosecutor do we see more of that I'm  for the prosecution side in part two I  mean frankly we gave the prosecutor  whatever airtime was available to us I  mean we looked for creative ways to  include the point of view of the  prosecutor who declined to sit down with  us I had written to Ken Kratz in 2006 to  invite him to participate never received  a direct response to my letter but you  know we were fortunate in that so many  events were playing out publicly so we  could film those and you know make it  clear what Ken Kratz his perspective was  on the story mentally when you think  about it though do you think about it as  a documentary where you're trying to put  things before people with with no  fingerprints of your own on it or do you  think of it as as a storytelling  exercise in which you're basically  advocating for an argument I think the  answer might be neither you know because  documentary is not journalism it is an  art form and you know this is a  narrative documentary so we had chosen  Steven Avery as our main character and  we had chosen him because of his  incredibly unique status as someone who  had been failed by the system in the in  the mid 80s and now was stepping back  into it you know and in that 20 year  time span you know there have been  advances in DNA  there had been legislative reforms and  there was a lot of talk about you know  because wrong you've been falsely  accused before and falsely imprisoned  yeah 18 years yes yes and so it was sort  of an opportunity to hold a mirror up  and to watch the investigation and the  prosecution play out and the question at  hand was how will he be treated and  accused one of the other criticisms  criticisms guys was about the victim we  don't hear much in part one about Teresa  Halbach you know a woman lost her life  under still mysterious circumstances  some say is she addressed at all in part  - absolutely I mean do we hear from her  family or well both in Part one and Part  two we invited the hall Bucs to  participate and in both instances they  declined and you know I just want to say  we absolutely understand that I mean  they endured an unspeakable tragedy and  you know I would imagine too that even  participating could you know create more  pain for them so we understand that they  declined we respect that and we in light  of that I mean regardless of whether  they participated or not our job was  tell to tell the story in the most  responsible way that we could so we  sought to include a range of voices when  the hall bucks declined to participate  we used archival materials to show you  know the the distressed people were in  when they thought that Teresa was in  jeopardy just when she was missing they  were volunteer searchers who set out to  look for her you had a lot of people  talking after part one and a lot more  people and I'm sure we'll be talking and  watching part 2 thank you so much for  joining us we appreciate it thank you  thank you thank you and you can catch  all 10 episodes of making a murderer  part 2 on Netflix starting tomorrow  you're watching CBS this morning we'll  be right back

